[17592186523923 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186524104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523923 #edb/ref 1085 "![](images/d4/d4bff62d0370b252f4bbbbace8503b225d71b05388f67038dff60d10a33a00ac.png)\n\nConsider the diagram shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523923 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "f93bccbd-7e87-5446-93d1-f17e4bdb0319" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523923 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186524079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523921 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167073" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523928 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\n$x\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0bc94237-c7e5-59e2-b81f-4ca609b2837a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523928 #edb/ref 1074 "Previously, we found that $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has equation\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0.$ So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ 0 & =-x+7 \\\\ x & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=7.$ The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(7,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523815 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523881 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167074" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the equation of $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ in **standard form**.\n\nThe equation is \n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}\n$y=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3fd08c56-40e8-5db2-852c-0b5043fc5d70" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523946 #edb/ref 1074 "Our goal is to write the equation in the form \n\n$$\nax+by=c,\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors and $a\\ge 0.$\n\nWe begin by moving all of the variables to the left-hand side and all of the constants to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x+y & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x+3y & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $a,b,$ and $c$ are integers with no common factors, and $a\\ge 0.$ So, we are done!\n\nThe standard form of the line is \n\n$$\nx+3y=7.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167071" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $y$-intercept of $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ and express the equation of this line in slope-intercept form.\n\nThe $y$-intercept of the line is $b\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n\nThe equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a3e6aca-bc4b-546e-bf1b-908fe05212b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523950 #edb/ref 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nAs we found previously, the slope is $m=-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+b \\\\ 1 & =-(\\frac{1}{3})(4)+b \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{4}{3}+b \\\\ 1+\\frac{4}{3} & =b \\\\ b & =\\frac{7}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523953 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523953 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167069" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523953 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523953 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the length of $\\overset{―}{AB}?$\n\n$|\\overset{―}{AB}|\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523953 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53008a1b-048b-5530-aee9-c37309ee0c8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523953 #edb/ref 1074 "From the picture, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{AB},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\overset{―}{AB}| & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4-(-2))^{2}+(1-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{36+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523860 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167067" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the $xy$-coordinates of the point $A?$\n\n$A\\,($\n{{field-1}}\n$,\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n$)$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d427fb2-847d-5c45-9124-9759bd9b4b84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523959 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the coordinates of the point, we inspect its position along the $x$- and $y$-axes.\n\n* The point is $2$ units to the *left* of the origin, in the *negative* direction of the $x$-axis. So, its $x$-coordinate is $-2.$\n\n* The point is $3$ units *above* the origin, in the *positive* direction of the $y$-axis. So, its $y$-coordinate is $3.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of our point are $(-2,3).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523863 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the equations of the vertical and horizontal lines that pass through the point $B.$\n\nThe **equation** of the vertical line is \n{{field-1}}\n\nThe **equation** of the horizontal line is \n{{field-2}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6b73135a-679b-5521-bab1-ffd76e8e9cf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523961 #edb/ref 1074 "The coordinates of $B$ are $(4,1).$\n\n* Since the vertical line should pass through $(4,1)$, the $x$-value for every point on the line is $4.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\nx=4.\n$$\n\n* Since the horizontal line should pass through $(4,1)$, the $y$-value for every point on the line is $1.$ Hence, the equation of that line is \n\n$$\ny=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523988 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523988 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167075" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523988 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523988 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,-2)$ and is parallel to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523988 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a61b0f7-d3d4-5914-af7a-56cdc12fc887" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523988 #edb/ref 1074 "Since parallel lines have equal slopes, the slope of the new line should be equal to the slope of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB},$ i.e.,\n\n$$\nm=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, we want a line with the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3}$ that passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3,-2).$ \n\nUsing point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (x-3) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524010 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524010 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167076" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524010 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524010 #edb/ref 1071 "Write the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,8)$ and is perpendicular to $\\overset{↔}{AB}.$\n\nThe slope-intercept form of the equation is $y\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524010 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c73684cd-cbd4-53ce-a02e-b8f120478413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524010 #edb/ref 1074 "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other. Therefore, since $\\overset{↔}{AB}$ has the slope $m=-\\frac{1}{3},$ our perpendicular line must have a slope of \n\n$$\n{m}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{1}{3})}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, our perpendicular line has the slope ${m}_{1}=3$ and passes through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,8).$ \n\nFinally, using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}_{1}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-8 & =3(x-2) \\\\ y-8 & =3x-6 \\\\ y & =3x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524019 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524019 #edb/ref 1069 "q-167070" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524019 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524019 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the slope $m$ of the line $\\overset{↔}{AB}?$\n\n$m\\,=\\,$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524019 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "db023775-f57a-5eec-8870-ad9e9767c00a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524019 #edb/ref 1074 "As we found previously, the points $A$ and $B$ have coordinates $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,3)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,1),$ respectively.\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-3}{4-(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524045 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524045 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1d578ebe-cf01-5882-a3ba-912891fd208c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524045 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523928 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524049 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524050 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524049 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31a02459-846b-594d-a08e-bf91b9b551fd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524049 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524050 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524067 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524050 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "31c22abb-16d4-5821-a52d-1bfe8c5323e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524050 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524055 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524082 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524055 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "418a9026-c9a0-531e-97f8-562e071db821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524055 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524059 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524059 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52217a6b-1ed6-54f2-b306-9197c33edb1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524059 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523953 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524067 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6cdce9b7-05c1-5e9f-8efe-1d31cef1a31b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524067 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523930 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524068 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524068 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71980436-9f6b-5480-98ee-52818effe5ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524068 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523961 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524070 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524104 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74402793-00a4-5df0-a606-8d38ba00eb3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186524019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524079 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524068 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524079 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96b65a37-e583-5995-a1bf-702398f75b01" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524082 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9eabdcab-40d8-5341-9749-8c6cf187dc81" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524082 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186524010 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524086 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524086 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a851ed19-5a59-5d18-994d-da92c50c5ecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524086 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524100 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9af0c7e-4cfc-5bcf-90d9-b4e076c38683" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524100 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523922 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524104 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186524045 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524104 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "eab2a3f1-57e3-5759-a9d3-a8cd3ff78ee3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186524104 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186523950 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
